  "notes": "The live-instrument end of this axis, and the only entry here with genuine changes rather than a loop drone. The research says \"real harmony… genuine changes rather than loop drones\", so the progressions run 4–8 bars with open voicings and no `impliedByRiff`, and the bass is `independent_riff` at `followRootsProb` 0.4 with the widest passing-tone set on this axis — a live electric player \"with real melodic lines and space\". The percussion lanes carry the hand percussion from his instrument collection. ⛔ **This model replaces the genre's dusty/jazzy/hard moods with its own, deliberately.** The research states two backbeat classes for one producer — straight 2&4 for the hip-hop cuts and a one-drop with the backbeat on beat 3 for the reggae-derived ones — and `session.halfTime` is the only honest way to say \"snare on 3\". Keeping the inherited moods would have left that unreachable. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it medium-high: the live-instrument approach is first-person, the numbers are catalog-level. The specific inferred parameter is the 60/30/10 mode weighting — the research says the one-drop exists but not how much of the catalogue it is. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 50 to 47 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 48 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 3. ⚠ `countermelody.fillGapsOnly` returned to true. With it off and a density of 0.6, the counter plays THROUGH the melody rather than around it, and coherence.rs measures that as the counter sounding the melody own pitch at the same moment — a second lead rather than an answer. The register offset and the styles are untouched; what changed is that it waits its turn.",
